## 1. High-Level Architecture

### BFF Pattern (Change #1, #2)

The browser **never** talks directly to FastAPI. All requests — including the SSE stream — flow through Next.js Route Handlers. Each Route Handler mints a short-lived (5–15 min) **HS256 JWT** server-side using the shared `BACKEND_SECRET` and forwards it to FastAPI in an `Authorization: Bearer` header.

```
Browser
  │
  │  (session cookie, HTTP-only)
  ▼
Next.js App (port 3000)
  ├── /app pages            ← Server + Client Components
  ├── /app/api/*            ← Route Handlers (BFF proxy)
  │     ├── mint HS256 JWT  ← BACKEND_SECRET (server-side only)
  │     ├── proxy → FastAPI ← BACKEND_URL (server-side only)
  │     └── /api/scans/[id]/stream  ← proxies SSE, or uses
  │                                    @microsoft/fetch-event-source
  └── /app/api/auth/[...nextauth]  ← NextAuth GitHub OAuth
           │
           └── stores GitHub token in server-side session
                 (never exposed to browser)
                            │
                            ▼
                       FastAPI (port 8000)
                            │
                    ┌───────┴────────┐
                    │                │
              PostgreSQL         Redis
              (SQLite MVP)    (BackgroundTasks MVP)
                                     │
                                     ▼
                              LangGraph Agent
                         ┌─────────────────────┐
                         │ Ingest → Scan        │
                         │  ↓                   │
                         │ Triage               │
                         │  ↓                   │
                         │ [per-finding loop]   │
                         │  Fix → Verify → ···  │
                         │  ↓                   │
                         │ Report               │
                         └─────────────────────┘
                                     │
                              Docker Sandbox
                         (install stage + scan/test stage)
```

### SSE Streaming (Change #2)

The native browser `EventSource` API cannot send custom headers, so the SSE stream is proxied through a Next.js Route Handler using `@microsoft/fetch-event-source`. The Route Handler maintains the connection to FastAPI and re-streams events to the browser. `Last-Event-ID` is forwarded so replay works transparently.

### Docker Architecture & Socket Decision (Change #15)

**The Docker socket is NOT mounted into any container that handles untrusted input.** Instead, the FastAPI backend process (running on the host or in its own container) calls the Docker SDK directly to spawn sandbox containers. This means:

- FastAPI container itself never touches the Docker socket (or it has the socket but is trusted and never processes untrusted code directly).
- Sandbox containers receive only a copy of the workspace directory via a bind mount scoped to that scan's temp directory.
- **Risk acknowledged**: if the FastAPI host is compromised, an attacker could spawn arbitrary containers. Mitigations: run FastAPI as a non-root user, use AppArmor/seccomp on the sandbox image, and keep the Docker socket accessible only to the trusted FastAPI service account.

